\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The Euclidean Clifford form of the lattice Dirac operator}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{The operators #{\gamma_j} of \ref{fdip-000R} are self-adjoint and
satisfy
##{\gamma_i\gamma_j+\gamma_j\gamma_i=2\delta_{ij}I_S.}
\ref{fdip-00L8} proves that they represent the spatial quadratic
form #{q_{\mathrm{sp}}}, with #{\gamma_j^2=I_S} and hence
#{q_{\mathrm{sp}}(\varepsilon_j)=1}. The covariant operator of
\ref{fdip-009O}, its Fourier symbol, and the Wilson blocks of
\ref{fdip-009V} use this Euclidean real form. The comparison
#{q_{\mathrm{sp}}(i\varepsilon_1)=-1} in \ref{fdip-00LB} is a value
of the same polynomial. It is not the generator substitution below.}

\p{In the complexification, the substitution #{\gamma_j\mapsto i\gamma_j}
replaces each square by its negative:
##{(i\gamma_j)^2=-I_S.}
If #{\gamma_j} is self-adjoint, then #{i\gamma_j} is skew-adjoint.
That substitution is not an identification of these Euclidean
operators with the [Lorentz-sign gamma algebra](fcap-002U).
\ref{fdip-000R} records that algebra as a different choice of
form, scalar field and representation. These notes make no claim
that the Lorentz-sign form is physically wrong.}

\p{The factor #{i} in the symbol #{i\sum_j\Gamma_j\sin p_j} is the
Fourier multiplier of the skew-adjoint centered difference. It is
not the substitution #{\gamma_j\mapsto i\gamma_j}. The operator
#{D_U} is skew-adjoint, so #{iD_U} is self-adjoint. Replacing the
skew Wilson symbol by that self-adjoint multiple changes the
kernel calculation of \ref{fdip-009T}: the cross term with the real
Wilson coefficient no longer cancels. That replacement is not the
Clifford substitution above.}
